# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the Linux tsan job
# (.github/workflows/sanitize.yml, cmake/sanitize.cmake, tests/tsan.cmake).
#
# STARTS EMPTY OF OUR CODE, AND STAYS THAT WAY. Every `called_from_lib` line below
# names a library this repository does not build, and hides a report only when that
# library is on the report's stack. Nothing names anything in src/, in a test, or in
# a vendored library compiled here (imgui, glfw, pffft): a race in those is a
# finding, not noise. A race in our code is fixed, not listed. THE ONE EXCEPTION is
# two named races in the vendored PortAudio, at the end of this file, each shown to
# be benign by construction and each matched by the exact name of the thing it is
# about, not by a library.
#
# WHY THIRD-PARTY LIBRARIES NEED THEM AT ALL. ThreadSanitizer sees only what is
# instrumented and what it can intercept (pthread calls, atomics it knows). A
# library built without it synchronises through its own atomics, futexes and fences,
# which it cannot see, so an access of ours to memory such a library hands over
# looks unsynchronised when it is not.
#
# The first run (37355910809) was the first Linux run to check them against. It showed
# that the libunwind, libpulsecommon and PortAudio lines below were missing; it did not
# exercise every other line here (libasound only appears behind the pulse report, and
# nothing reached the display libraries), so those stay until a log shows them unneeded.

# ALSA: the sound server's plugin layer and pcm locking use futex-based waits that
# are invisible to the sanitizer; our audio sink writes to a pcm another thread of
# the library services.
called_from_lib:libasound.so.2

# Mesa's software renderer (llvmpipe, via libgallium or swrast_dri) and the LLVM it
# JIT-compiles with: its own worker-thread pool and queues, running under every
# test that opens the hidden window under xvfb.
called_from_lib:libgallium
called_from_lib:swrast_dri.so
called_from_lib:libLLVM
called_from_lib:libGLX_mesa.so.0
called_from_lib:libEGL_mesa.so.0

# The X11 and Wayland client libraries: their display-connection locking is
# internal (XInitThreads / the wl_display queue lock).
called_from_lib:libX11.so
called_from_lib:libxcb.so
called_from_lib:libwayland-client.so.0

# PulseAudio's client library. ALSA's "default" pcm resolves to its pulse plugin on
# the runner, and opening it (PortAudio's BuildDeviceList, every test that touches
# the sound sink) starts a threaded main loop inside libpulsecommon. That thread
# allocates the library's own mutexes (pa_xmalloc) and hands them to the thread that
# opened the pcm through the library's atomics and an eventfd, none of which
# ThreadSanitizer sees. Reported as "data race ... in pa_mutex_lock" in 15 of the
# first run's tests: the two accesses are the malloc on the loop thread and the
# pthread_mutex_lock on ours, on a 40-byte block the library allocated, both made
# from inside the library. Nothing of ours is on either side.
called_from_lib:libpulsecommon

# libunwind. The hang watchdog and the crash path capture another thread's stack by
# signalling it and walking from the handler (unw_step). libunwind initialises its
# signal mask once, under its own lock, on the first thread to unwind (sigfillset),
# and every later unwind reads it (sigprocmask) with no lock, trusting the library's
# own initialised flag, which is an atomic ThreadSanitizer cannot see because the
# library is not instrumented. Reported as "data race ... in _ULx86_64_step" in 9 of
# the first run's tests (test_excuse_cap, test_hang_signature and test_watchdog_startup
# among them): both accesses are interceptor calls made from inside libunwind, to
# libunwind's own global (libunwind.so.8+0x16a80), not to any memory of ours.
called_from_lib:libunwind.so.8

# THE TWO UPSTREAM PORTAUDIO RACES. PortAudio is vendored (third_party/portaudio, at
# the pinned upstream commit) and compiled here WITH instrumentation, so unlike a
# system library its races are ours to answer for; these two are real data races in
# the C sense, are in upstream PortAudio as pinned, and are harmless by construction.
# They are matched by exact name, so a race on anything else in PortAudio - its
# callback ring above all - is still reported. The alternative to listing them is to
# patch the vendored tree (THIRD_PARTY.md records its patches); that has not been done
# because pa_unix_util.c is not compiled on Windows, so the change could not be built
# where this repository is developed.
#
#   paUtilErr_   - a file-static scratch int, `static PaError paUtilErr_` in
#                  pa_unix_util.h, assigned the result of a pthread call by
#                  PA_ENSURE / PA_ENSURE_SYSTEM / PA_ASSERT_CALL and compared in the
#                  same statement. Every thread writes it; the only value ever stored
#                  by a call that succeeds is 0, and a failed pthread_mutex_unlock is
#                  a programming error PortAudio asserts on. Reported in 5 tests as a
#                  write/write race in PaUnixMutex_Unlock.
race:paUtilErr_
#   PaUnixThread_NotifyParent - sets `parentWaiting = 0` under the thread's mutex;
#                  PaUnixThread_New reads that flag once without the mutex (a double-
#                  checked wait: if it reads a stale 1 it takes the mutex and
#                  re-reads in the while loop, if it reads 0 the child has already
#                  signalled), so the unlocked read is a hint that is always
#                  re-verified. Reported in test_audio_open_log. The function is a
#                  dozen lines whose only shared field is that flag.
race:PaUnixThread_NotifyParent

# DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED.
#   libusb       - not linked: the USB drivers use usbfs directly (src/usb).
#   PortAudio's ring buffer - its callback thread and our writer share it, and its
#                  ordering is made with full memory barriers (PaUtil_FullMemoryBarrier),
#                  which ThreadSanitizer does not model as synchronisation. No run has
#                  reported it yet (every test stopped on an earlier report first, so
#                  that is not evidence it is clean). If a log reports that ring, the
#                  line to add is `race:PaUtil_` (the ring's functions), with that
#                  report as the reason; it is not added on a guess.
